  "tour": {
    "title": "When Prayer Goes Quiet: A Listening Tour Through Fresh Grief",
    "intro": "You are only three weeks into a loss, so this tour does not begin by trying to fix your prayer life or explain your mother’s death. It begins with the rawness itself: a broken heart that feels unimportant, Jesus weeping beside mourners, lament as a way to speak honestly when prayer feels impossible, and finally the question of where God is when the darkness does not lift. Listen slowly and stop whenever you need to; you do not have to turn any of these clips into a prayer.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:f189e210-8df4-11ee-b97f-09218d3fce01",
        "startSec": 136,
        "endSec": 370,
        "why": "Begin here because the clip meets the exact shame and distance that can come with fresh grief. Wright says, “This is not selfish and it is not unimportant,” and even, “God loves your broken heart as much as he loved your unbroken heart before.” He does not rush past the sorrow; he points toward Jesus in Gethsemane and at Lazarus’s tomb, then toward Psalms that can hold doubt and darkness when you cannot find your own words.",
        "episodeTitle": "#196 Pain and suffering Part 2 (Classic)",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR3879333769.mp3?updated=1749653312",
        "durationSec": 1455,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:a1a084d1-com-06ada9f3-4c3f-3851-9ba5-35f817a4ee6f",
        "startSec": 1307,
        "endSec": 1507,
        "why": "Stay with the scene at Lazarus’s tomb. The commentator lets Mary say the words grief often wants to say—“Lord, if you had been here, our brother wouldn’t have died”—and describes Jesus as emotionally “deeply agitated” by death before arriving at the plain sentence, “Jesus wept.” This clip gives you presence rather than a neat explanation: Jesus does not scold the mourners for their tears or hurry them past the horror of loss.",
        "episodeTitle": "John 11:1-54",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/rhu2ccvr4rgf5c54/john_111-54bhwxv.mp3",
        "durationSec": 2212,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:67576c87-242c-42e5-a03d-63663cd5d2d6",
        "startSec": 1557,
        "endSec": 1830,
        "why": "Now hear Psalm 22 as permission not to pretend. The speakers note that David gives “11 verses of just here’s what I’m experiencing” before his first request, “Be not far from me.” They warn against either turning away from God or faking your emotions, and say of someone whose mother has just died, “it’s a godly thing to be honest with God about your emotions.” The clip treats bringing the pain itself to God as a form of prayer.",
        "episodeTitle": "Psalm 22: Lament Psalms (w/ Cameron Cole)",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG7303573431.mp3?updated=1686154567",
        "durationSec": 2801,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:efa1c31b-com-675da7aa-6691-5371-9a69-ace341ca3888",
        "startSec": 1101,
        "endSec": 1260,
        "why": "End with the question of God’s location in the silence, without pretending to solve why this happened. Wright says that “the Spirit groans within us,” acknowledges a Psalm that ends with “darkness is my only companion,” and then describes God as “the God of all compassion, who comes to be with us in that darkness.” It leaves the mystery intact, but it refuses to equate your inability to pray with God’s absence.",
        "episodeTitle": "#33 Tom on Coronavirus, self-isolating and praying through crisis",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR8934467240.mp3?updated=1736469733",
        "durationSec": 2321,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You may not finish this tour feeling better, and that is not its measure. For now, it is enough to let these voices hold a little space around your grief: your heart is not an inconvenience, lament need not be polished, and wordless darkness is not something you have to face alone. If a clip is too much today, stopping is part of listening too.",
    "totalSeconds": 866
  },
